13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B2}
White’s first move that forces the kill is B2. After B1 is still open the black stones A2, B3 and C2 share the common liberty B2 with the C1-C2 group, and the whole left side of the black shape is held together by that point. Playing B2 takes away the last joint liberty of A2-B3-C2, and also cuts off the connection that black would need in order to make the connection A2-B2-B3. 

After W B2 the black stones A2, B3, C2, C1 all have only the outside liberties A1,A3,A4 left, and the white wall on the right B5-C3-C4-D1-D2-D5 already controls A4 and the D side. Black’s only hope is to answer at A3, but W A1 then follows and the net closes on B3-B4, and the C1-C2 stones are left with no eye. With correct follow-up at A1 and A3 white captures the connected black group regardless of whether black tries to reinforce at A1, A3 or A4 first, so B2 is a winning first move to start the capture.

11.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B1}

After White B1 the black three-stone net C1-C2 has the single remaining liberty B2. 
B2 is now a self-atari for Black:  B2 is surrounded by A2,B3,C2 black and B1 white, so Black cannot play B2 and Black cannot capture the newly placed B1 because B1 still has the liberty A1.  Hence Black cannot defend C1-C2 and White captures on the next move with B2.  The capture is forced because the other liberty of C1-C2 is now unplayable for Black, so B1 is the unique forcing first move.

